# Functional Programming Practices

> **IMPORTANT**: When applying these functional programming practices, you MUST also follow the documentation-and-comments-and-logs.mdc rules for all documentation, comments, and logging.

## Core Principles

### 1. Pure Functions
- Functions should always return the same output for the same input
- No side effects (no modifying external state)
- No reliance on external state
- Easy to test and reason about

```language
// Good
function calculateTotal(items) {
    return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0);
}

// Bad
let total = 0;
function calculateTotal(items) {
    for (const item of items) {
        total += item.price;  // Side effect: modifies external state
    }
    return total;
}
```

### 2. Immutability
- Data should be immutable by default
- Create new objects instead of modifying existing ones
- Use immutable data structures where possible
- Avoid mutable state

```language
// Good
function updateUser(user, newName) {
    return { ...user, name: newName };  // Create new object
}

// Bad
function updateUser(user, newName) {
    user.name = newName;  // Mutates existing object
    return user;
}
```

### 3. Function Composition
- Break down complex operations into smaller functions
- Compose functions to build more complex operations
- Use higher-order functions
- Chain operations together

```language
// Good
function processData(data) {
    return pipe(
        data,
        cleanInput,
        validateData,
        transformData,
        formatOutput
    );
}

// Bad
function processData(data) {
    // All operations in one function
    const cleaned = cleanInput(data);
    const validated = validateData(cleaned);
    const transformed = transformData(validated);
    return formatOutput(transformed);
}
```

### 4. Declarative Programming
- Focus on what to do, not how to do it
- Use built-in functions and operators
- Avoid explicit loops where possible
- Use functional operations (map, filter, reduce)

```language
// Good
function getActiveUsers(users) {
    return users.filter(user => user.isActive);
}

// Bad
function getActiveUsers(users) {
    const activeUsers = [];
    for (const user of users) {
        if (user.isActive) {
            activeUsers.push(user);
        }
    }
    return activeUsers;
}
```

## Best Practices

### 1. Function Design

#### Single Responsibility
- Each function should do one thing
- Keep functions small and focused
- Use descriptive names
- Avoid mixing concerns

```language
// Good
function calculateTax(price, rate) {
    return price * rate;
}

function calculateTotal(price, tax) {
    return price + tax;
}

// Bad
function processOrder(price, rate) {
    const tax = price * rate;
    const total = price + tax;
    return total;
}
```

#### Function Purity
- Avoid side effects
- Don't modify input parameters
- Don't rely on external state
- Make dependencies explicit

```language
// Good
function formatName(first, last) {
    return `${first} ${last}`.trim();
}

// Bad
function formatName(first, last) {
    global.formattedNames.push(`${first} ${last}`.trim());  // Side effect
    return `${first} ${last}`.trim();
}
```

### 2. Data Handling

#### Immutable Data Structures
- Use immutable collections
- Create new objects instead of modifying
- Use copy-on-write patterns
- Avoid mutable state

```language
// Good
function updateUserList(users, newUser) {
    return [...users, newUser];  // Create new array
}

// Bad
function updateUserList(users, newUser) {
    users.push(newUser);  // Mutates array
    return users;
}
```

#### Data Transformation
- Use map, filter, reduce
- Avoid imperative loops
- Chain operations
- Keep transformations pure

```language
// Good
function processItems(items) {
    return items
        .filter(item => item.isValid)
        .map(item => item.transform())
        .reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.value, 0);
}

// Bad
function processItems(items) {
    let total = 0;
    for (const item of items) {
        if (item.isValid) {
            const transformed = item.transform();
            total += transformed.value;
        }
    }
    return total;
}
```

### 3. Error Handling

#### Functional Error Handling
- Use Either/Option types
- Avoid exceptions for control flow
- Handle errors explicitly
- Keep error handling pure

```language
// Good
function divide(a, b) {
    return b !== 0 
        ? Either.right(a / b)
        : Either.left("Division by zero");
}

// Bad
function divide(a, b) {
    try {
        return a / b;
    } catch (error) {
        return null;
    }
}
```

#### Error Propagation
- Use monadic operations
- Chain error handling
- Keep error handling pure
- Make error cases explicit

```language
// Good
function processData(data) {
    return Either.right(data)
        .map(validate)
        .map(transform)
        .map(save);
}

// Bad
function processData(data) {
    try {
        const validated = validate(data);
        const transformed = transform(validated);
        return save(transformed);
    } catch (error) {
        return null;
    }
}
```

### 4. Testing

#### Pure Function Testing
- Test input/output pairs
- No need for setup/teardown
- Easy to test edge cases
- Test pure functions in isolation

```language
// Good
function testCalculateTotal() {
    const items = [
        { price: 10 },
        { price: 20 }
    ];
    assert(calculateTotal(items) === 30);
}

// Bad
function testCalculateTotal() {
    global.total = 0;  // Setup required
    const items = [
        { price: 10 },
        { price: 20 }
    ];
    assert(calculateTotal(items) === 30);
}
```

#### Property-Based Testing
- Test properties, not examples
- Use random inputs
- Verify invariants
- Test edge cases automatically

```language
// Good
@given(arrays(integers()))
function testSumCommutative(numbers) {
    assert(sum(numbers) === sum(reverse(numbers)));
}

// Bad
function testSum() {
    assert(sum([1, 2, 3]) === 6);  // Only tests one case
}
```

## Implementation Guidelines

### 1. Code Organization

#### Module Structure
- Group related functions
- Keep modules focused
- Use clear exports
- Maintain separation of concerns

```language
// Good
// mathUtils.js
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
export function subtract(a, b) { return a - b; }
export function multiply(a, b) { return a * b; }

// Bad
// utils.js
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
export function formatName(name) { return name.trim(); }
export function saveToDb(data) { return db.save(data); }
```

#### Function Composition
- Use composition over inheritance
- Keep functions small
- Use higher-order functions
- Chain operations

```language
// Good
function processUser(user) {
    return pipe(
        user,
        validateUser,
        enrichUserData,
        formatUserOutput
    );
}

// Bad
class UserProcessor {
    process(user) {
        this.validate(user);
        this.enrich(user);
        return this.format(user);
    }
}
```

### 2. Performance

#### Lazy Evaluation
- Use generators
- Avoid unnecessary computation
- Chain operations efficiently
- Process data on demand

```language
// Good
function* processLargeDataset(items) {
    for (const item of items) {
        if (item.isValid) {
            const transformed = transform(item);
            if (transformed.isReady) {
                yield transformed;
            }
        }
    }
}

// Bad
function processLargeDataset(items) {
    const validItems = items.filter(item => item.isValid);
    const transformed = validItems.map(transform);
    return transformed.filter(item => item.isReady);
}
```

#### Memoization
- Cache expensive computations
- Keep memoization pure
- Use appropriate cache keys
- Consider memory usage

```language
// Good
const memoize = (fn) => {
    const cache = new Map();
    return (arg) => {
        if (!cache.has(arg)) {
            cache.set(arg, fn(arg));
        }
        return cache.get(arg);
    };
};

// Bad
const cache = {};
function fibonacci(n) {
    if (n in cache) return cache[n];
    if (n < 2) return n;
    const result = fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
    cache[n] = result;
    return result;
}
```

## Documentation Integration

### 1. Function Documentation
- Document all functions with clear purpose and behavior
- Include input/output contracts
- Document side effects (if any)
- Document error cases and handling

```language
/**
 * Calculates the total price of items with tax.
 * 
 * @param {Array<Item>} items - List of items to calculate total for
 * @param {number} taxRate - Tax rate to apply (0-1)
 * @returns {number} Total price including tax
 * @throws {Error} If tax rate is invalid
 */
function calculateTotalWithTax(items, taxRate) {
    // Implementation
}
```

### 2. Module Documentation
- Document module purpose and responsibilities
- List exported functions and their purposes
- Document module-level invariants
- Include usage examples

```language
/**
 * Math utilities for financial calculations.
 * 
 * This module provides pure functions for common financial calculations.
 * All functions are immutable and have no side effects.
 * 
 * @module mathUtils
 */

/**
 * Adds two numbers with proper decimal handling.
 * @param {number} a - First number
 * @param {number} b - Second number
 * @returns {number} Sum of a and b
 */
export function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}
```

### 3. Type Documentation
- Document custom types and their purposes
- Include type constraints and invariants
- Document type relationships
- Include usage examples

```language
/**
 * Represents a financial transaction.
 * 
 * @typedef {Object} Transaction
 * @property {string} id - Unique transaction identifier
 * @property {number} amount - Transaction amount
 * @property {string} currency - Currency code
 * @property {Date} timestamp - Transaction timestamp
 */

/**
 * Creates a new transaction.
 * 
 * @param {string} id - Transaction ID
 * @param {number} amount - Transaction amount
 * @param {string} currency - Currency code
 * @returns {Transaction} New transaction object
 */
function createTransaction(id, amount, currency) {
    return {
        id,
        amount,
        currency,
        timestamp: new Date()
    };
}
```

